{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"buiqw2hzy","tool_use_id":"toolu_016MRAnwTygNSE7TTEPcR3HG","status":"completed","output_file":"/tmp/claude-441489/-scratch-users-kushinm-auto-psych-outer-loop-live-runs-run3-repo/23d9a74f-128c-47a3-9ace-dfe5c6492fac/tasks/buiqw2hzy.output","summary":"Background command \"cd /scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/run3/data/subjective_randomness/experiment3/model_loop; D=iter_0/candidate_4_repair_1\necho switch_gist_own_length_scale > $D/model_name.txt\ncat > $D/hypothesis.md <<'EOF'\nRefinement of the incumbent `gamblers_run_second_order_side_habit`: people still judge a sequence random to the extent their own second-order, gambler's-fallacy model of a fair coin explains it better than the regular generators they suspect (a switch-biased coin, a biased coin, a repeating short motif with slips, with each person's own motif suspicion), with person-specific sensitivity and side habit. The single change is that the person's quick impression of how often a sequence switches (judged against their own believed switch rate of chance) is weighed on its own length scale, separate from the Bayesian weighing of the regular explanations: the switch-count impression is discounted (or amplified) with sequence length by its own fitted power, so its pull relative to the pattern evidence differs between short and long pairs — addressing the critique that in very short pairs people prefer the more-switching sequence less than the incumbent's single length scaling predicts.\nEOF\npython3 - <<'EOF'\np=\"iter_0/candidate_4_repair_1/candidate.py\"\ns=open(\"models/gamblers_run_second_order_side_habit.py\").read()\ns=s.replace('\"\"\"Gambler\\'s-fallacy run-length belief added','''\"\"\"Switch-rate impression weighed on its own length scale.\n\nRefinement of `gamblers_run_second_order_side_habit` (described below). Single\nchange: the person-specific switch-belief term w_i * switch_diff is scaled by\n(n / 5) ** -gamma_w with its own fitted exponent gamma_w, instead of sharing the\nexponent gamma of the Bayesian chance-vs-regular evidence. So the relative pull\nof the switch-count impression versus the pattern evidence changes with\nsequence length (addresses the critique that people prefer more switches in\nlength-2/3 pairs less than predicted).\n\nDescription of the incumbent:\nGambler's-fallacy run-length belief added''',1)\nold=\"    eta = (w[pid] * switch_diff + beta[pid] * (log_chance2_diff - log_regular_diff)) * length_scale\\n\"\nassert old in s\ns=s.replace(old,'''    # Own length scale for the switch-count impression.\n    gamma_w = pm.Normal(\"gamma_w\", mu=0.5, sigma=0.5)\n    switch_scale = pt.exp(-gamma_w * pt.log(trial_len / 5.0))\n    eta = (\n        w[pid] * switch_diff * switch_scale\n        + beta[pid] * (log_chance2_diff - log_regular_diff) * length_scale\n    )\n''')\nopen(p,\"w\").write(s)\nEOF\ncd /scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/runs/run3/repo && /scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/venv/bin/python -m src.pipelines.inner_loop.check_candidate --candidate-dir /scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/run3/data/subjective_randomness/experiment3/model_loop/iter_0/candidate_4_repair_1 --responses /scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/run3/data/subjective_randomness/experiment3/model_loop/responses.csv 2>&1 | tail -5\" completed (exit code 0)","uuid":"267b077c-e927-4656-bd33-6dd6f5786060","session_id":"23d9a74f-128c-47a3-9ace-dfe5c6492fac"}
